<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gonçalo da Silveira</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1526">1526</a>–<a href="/years/1561">1561</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gonçalo da Silveira was a Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionary who in 1560–1561 travelled to the court of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwene-mutapa">Mwene Mutapa</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> and baptised the king and members of his family. Muslim traders at the court persuaded the king that the missionary was a spy and sorcerer, and Silveira was strangled in March 1561. His death was later used to justify the Portuguese expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-barreto">Francisco Barreto</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/goncalo-da-silveira.jpg" alt="Gonçalo da Silveira" width="320" height="492" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Author : Adrien Melaer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Martyrdom_by_strangulation_of_Jesuit_Father_Gon%C3%A7alo_da_Silveira_in_Monomotapa_1561.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Silveira was born at Almeirim, about 64 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, on 23 February 1526, the tenth child of Luís da Silveira, first count of Sortelha, and Beatriz Coutinho. Orphaned in infancy, he was brought up by his sister Filipa de Vilhena and her husband, the Marquis of Távora. He was taught by the Friars Minor of Santa Margarida until 1542, then went to the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a>, where after little more than a year Father Miron received him into the Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1555 Silveira was appointed provincial superior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, an appointment approved by Ignatius Loyola a few months before his death, and he governed the province for three years. His successor, António Quadros, sent him to south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. He landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a> on 11 March 1560 and went on to Otongwe near Cape Correntes.</p><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa</a> court in 1561 his refusal of gifts of women, gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and land impressed the young Mwenemutapa Negomo, who invited him into his private quarters. Silveira gave him a painting of the Virgin Mary, which Negomo hung where he slept. After about a fortnight of instruction Negomo and his mother were baptised. Negomo's gift of 100 cows, which Silveira had killed and shared among the people, raised his standing, and he baptised hundreds of high-ranking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shona">Shona</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spirit-medium">Spirit mediums</a> and diviners, Muslim traders and unconverted officials saw him as a threat. In the traditional account, given by historians such as Mudenge, they told Negomo that Silveira was a spy preparing a Portuguese invasion and a wizard, and a group led by the mbokorume strangled him and threw his body into the Musengezi River. Gai Roufe, re-examining the evidence, argues that Silveira was understood as embodying a feared foreign spirit, Karuva or Dzivaguru. When Portuguese merchants warned of vengeance, Negomo blamed the Muslims and had those involved killed.</p><p>A cause for Silveira's beatification was opened in 1629, and he holds the title Servant of God. H. Rider Haggard based the character José Silvestre in King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>'s Mines (1885) on him, and Silveira House, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> development centre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>, bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gonçalo da Silveira” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1561 the Jesuit <b>Gonçalo da Silveira</b> briefly converted the reigning Mwene Mutapa to Christianity, only to be killed at the royal court soon afterwards.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mutapa-empire">Mutapa Empire · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Almeirim, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.22,-8.63&amp;year=1548" aria-label="Almeirim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4336 758 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gonçalo da Silveira"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4786 1028h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4786 1028h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.22,-8.63&amp;year=1548">What was here in 1548? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
